{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1041592-backwards-comaptibility","id":1041592,"title":"Backwards Comaptibility","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-01-31T17:50:19-08:00","id":1041592,"title":"Backwards Comaptibility","content":"How well do the 2E supernatural character books play with original nWoD core book? Is it relatively easy to fit them or do the assumptions that these make with the new rules not fit in to the old core book easily?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12885-e11even-e11even","name":"E11even-E11even","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12885_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T18:19:46-08:00","id":1041608,"title":"","content":"The second edition core books are self-contained, you don't need any other book to play Vampire, Werewolf, Mage, Beast, or Promethean beyond their core. Maybe Demon.<br /> <br /> So what exactly do you mean?<br /> <br /> Do you mean can you play a mortal with the new World of Darkness core next to a second edition vampire?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T18:23:48-08:00","id":1041612,"title":"","content":"I didn't know that they were self-contained. I thought that you needed the core rulebook to still play them, and I am having issues with the Chronicles of Darkness book, so I was thinking of seeing how my nWoD core book would hold up rules-wise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12885-e11even-e11even","name":"E11even-E11even","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12885_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T18:28:39-08:00","id":1041615,"title":"","content":"There was a lengthy transition process between first edition and second, and before second came there was the God-Machine Chronicle, which also had <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/114078/World-of-Darkness-GodMachine-Rules-Update\" target=\"_blank\">the God-Machine Rules Update</a>. It's a fairly brief file that updates the rules to what would become second edition. It doesn't deal with the monster making, and is more about how you would make and run a mortal character.<br /> <br /> Is there any particular part you're having trouble with?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T18:33:51-08:00","id":1041617,"title":"","content":"I am having issues with Investigation, Social Maneuvering, Chasing, and to an extent, Combat. I haven't gotten to Crafting yet, but I imagine I'll have issues with that as well. These systems all seem overly complicated and bogged down, and I don't see official replacements for them in the book, although I've heard people say they're optional. Am I missing the replacement systems?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12885-e11even-e11even","name":"E11even-E11even","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12885_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T18:37:20-08:00","id":1041621,"title":"","content":"They are optional, though suggested. The basic Investigation 'system' would be, 'Roll Wits+Investigation + or - a modifier.' And the basic Social System is, 'Roll Presence + Intimidation + or - a modifier.' Chase would probably be the same as it was in the first edition book. But basically you can skip over the lengthy 'systems' with a simple roll, though the specific Attribute + Skill dicepool may be different depending on the situation and characters involved.<br /> <br /> The big difference in combat is weapons add to damage, not to dicepools, and Athletics adds to Defense. So if I have a sword with a +3 weapon rating, and my dicepool of Strength and Weaponry is 7, and you have a Wits and Dexterity of 2, but Athletics of 3, that gives you 5 Defense. I roll my two dice, as 7-5=2. It doesn't look good, but I'm a golem and don't have Willpower to boost it. Still, I got 2 successes, and with my mighty sword that adds 3 damage, causing a whopping 5 Lethal. You're seriously hurt.<br /> <br /> The Storyteller knows you, and you're straight from D&amp;D and likely to keep fighting, so he unceremoniously gives you the Beaten Down Tilt. You can still Defend yourself, if you want, or you can attack, if you're willing to spend a Willpower. Or you can give me what I want, and you wisely decide to hand over the Vacuous Grimoire. You gain a Willpower and a Beat, let out a sigh of relief as I take the book and go. You can come after it again later, when you have buddies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-31-2017, 09:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T19:00:50-08:00","id":1041629,"title":"","content":"Alright, I feel ya. I am careful about replacing systems- especially ones that don't specifically list that they're optional and that are in depth- because that means a lot of things in the game could be balanced with them in mind. I got into the Storyteller System years ago because I love how it put story and characters in front of rules, but when I got to Investigation and Social Maneuvering in CoD, I felt that this was exactly the opposite of that. I really wish they'd state in the book that these systems are optional, because now I'm going to get that 'house rules' feeling whenever I use normal rolls, haha.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12885-e11even-e11even","name":"E11even-E11even","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12885_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T19:56:02-08:00","id":1041648,"title":"","content":"They're only optional in the sense that every rule is optional. Many creature powers and sub-systems are written from the view that these rules exist. You can ignore them but be aware that you'll then need to adapt powers etc. to the system you are using when you come across them.<br /> <br /> But as encouragement and counterpoint to your points, both Investigation and Social manoeuvring do put story and characters before rules by handing greater control to the player. SM in particular lets the player work out how they're going to approach a social challenge. You as Storyteller can do the quick method of saying 'this guy's resistant to your idea, but not completely against it, roll Attritbute + Skill - his resistance penalty to see if you can persuade him'. The nice thing about SM is that it lets the player say 'I'm going to tap into our shared interest of cars to bring him around. I'll roll Wits + Drive, and the Storyteller knows what the resistance penalty is because it's his Doors, and SM defines how you work out those. If it's an extended roll, such as the character develops an ongoing relationship to get the other person on side, SM has you covered. It tells you the interval between rolls, gives the player ways to reduce that interval, lets the player play to the character's strengths ('We've talked about cars enough, this week we're going hunting together, I'll roll Dex + Survival'), and gives clear guidance on what roll results mean. It's the same as if you make the player roll a different social test each time she encounters a guy, but this wraps it up into a nice bundle and gives power to the player.<br /> <br /> Same with investigations. If it's as simple as looking around a room to find which nightclub the killer has gone to, that's a quick roll and provide the clue. But if it's more complicated, and the characters need to work a little harder to keep chasing down the answers, it's again wrapped up the extended system in a nice bundle for you, which again gives the players greater input to play to their characters' strengths.<br /> <br /> Both these systems can look a little imposing from the outside, and having titles like 'Investigations' and 'Social Maneuvering' can make them seem strange and involved, but they're essentially what Storytellers have already been doing, just put all in one place for the players to find what they need.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T19:58:24-08:00","id":1041649,"title":"","content":"I haven't gotten around to using them but things keep popping up in my game that make me think, 'This would be better as some sort of segmented, more drawn-out sequence.' And the Investigation system would fit in nicely, or the Social one, as it is meant for applying 'persuasion or social pressure over time.' But for the most part I'm just using the usual roll-to-solve, as the players are still getting used to the new Mage system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-01T02:59:49-08:00","id":1041752,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>E11even-E11even</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1041592-backwards-comaptibility?p=1041629#post1041629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Alright, I feel ya. I am careful about replacing systems- especially ones that don't specifically list that they're optional and that are in depth- because that means a lot of things in the game could be balanced with them in mind. I got into the Storyteller System years ago because I love how it put story and characters in front of rules, but when I got to Investigation and Social Maneuvering in CoD, I felt that this was exactly the opposite of that. I really wish they'd state in the book that these systems are optional, because now I'm going to get that 'house rules' feeling whenever I use normal rolls, haha.</div> </div> </div> </div>If anything the Investigation rules give more power to players as they can spend a clue to get a known bonus on dice pools rather than depending on ST interpretation of how good a clue is. I'm not 100% on bord with how abstract it all feels to read but I suspect using it in play would help.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4714-live-bait","name":"Live Bait","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-01T09:11:48-08:00","id":1041865,"title":"","content":"The more I dwell on the systems, the more I understand the why of them, but not necessarily the how.<br /> It seems to me like they're in place to actually help balance out combat in the gameplay. Because even with heavy emphasis on social interactions and clue-gathering in WoD, combat always ends up with really deep rules and systems comparatively, because it's a matter of life and death, and this seems to help the game focus as much on mental and social gameplay. Which is good.<br /> <br /> I'm just still confused by it all, and I think the only real way to deal with it is to try it out. Although right now the abstraction of it all still bothers me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12885-e11even-e11even","name":"E11even-E11even","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12885_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-01T10:51:34-08:00","id":1041919,"title":"","content":"They're optional, in that it's always okay to use a single roll, or even to just agree as a group on what happens. The subsystems (all of them, including violence, experience, and even Integrity) are there to let you zoom in on parts of the story that are particularly interesting in the Chronicles of Darkness. As mentioned above, they also shift more influence over the unfolding story in the players' direction.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I'm not fond of acting like we're so embarrassed about having rules that we're subtly recommending that you don't use them, so they're not that kind of optional.<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT: Oh, and don't worry about that house rules feeling! The Storytelling system is designed from the get-go to be built and improvised on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":2,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","user":"Rose Bailey","date":". 02-01-2017, 01:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-01T20:21:09-08:00","id":1042204,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>E11even-E11even</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1041592-backwards-comaptibility?p=1041865#post1041865\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The more I dwell on the systems, the more I understand the why of them, but not necessarily the how.<br /> It seems to me like they're in place to actually help balance out combat in the gameplay. Because even with heavy emphasis on social interactions and clue-gathering in WoD, combat always ends up with really deep rules and systems comparatively, because it's a matter of life and death, and this seems to help the game focus as much on mental and social gameplay. Which is good.<br /> <br /> I'm just still confused by it all, and I think the only real way to deal with it is to try it out. Although right now the abstraction of it all still bothers me.</div> </div> </div> </div>The best thing to do is to start basic. For example, let's take Social Maneuvering. It seems complex, but start small. Create an NPC that has something one or more of your PCs wants. Give the NPC the lowest amount of Doors possible (Lowest of Resolve or Composure, don't worry about Vice or Aspirations yet) and maybe give him a Good Impression of the PCs to start (Maybe the PCs did something for the NPC in the past, and the NPC isn't so resistant). Have the PCs engage in a scene with the NPC to try to give an excellent or perfect first impression.This usually means looking good or doing something for the NPC's benefit. Then as they become chummy with the NPC, they can attempt to open the NPC's Doors to get what they want. Each Door is going to represent a level of hesitance on the NPC's part to give them what they want. Maybe a Door will involve the PCs going on a mission or doing a favor for the NPC, or it may just involve the PCs kissing ass, offering gifts and making promises. If the PCs open a door, then the NPC is more open to giving them what they want. If the PCs manage to open all the doors, then they get the prize, or an alternative but useful substitute (up to the ST).<br /> <br /> Once you are comfortable with Social Maneuvering, you can start making it more complex (Adding more Doors, or making situations more challenging, and maybe having your characters force open some doors). You don't have to use SM with every single social interaction, but use it at times to create drama, or when you don't think a reward should come easily. SM is also good for Down Time scenes if you want your PCs to get a hold of gear and items they can't just buy with Resources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5700-nuwisha-nutjob","name":"Nuwisha Nutjob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5700_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T18:07:34-08:00","id":1043284,"title":"","content":"Hey, I've been (and still am) extremely sick so I haven't had much energy to respond but I want to say thanks for everyone helping out in here. Advice and perspectives on these new rules are what I needed. I'm going to relook them over, but I'll also probably wade slowly into their integration in my game, and start off mostly using simple rolls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12885-e11even-e11even","name":"E11even-E11even","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12885_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}